    <body>
      <sec id="article-28218.s1" sec-type="Definition/Introduction">
        <title>Definition/Introduction</title>
        <p content-type="pubmed-excerpt">A radiopharmaceutical consists of 2 components: a radionuclide, which is a radioactive agent, and a pharmaceutical molecule, which acts as a tissue-localizing agent.&#x000a0;These compounds are used for diagnostic or therapeutic interventions.&#x000a0;Although the administration of radiopharmaceuticals is often systemic, they are likely to localize to specific tissues due to their biomolecular properties, ie, the areas of hyperintensity observed on positron emission tomography (PET) scans that indicate high tissue metabolic demand. In PET scans, positrons emitted by the radionuclide collide with electrons in the tissue, producing gamma rays that are detected by the scanner to create detailed images of metabolic activity. Because badiopharmaceuticals (the radionuclide component) actively emit radiation, their storage is more complex than that of non-radioactive pharmaceuticals.&#x000a0;Proper&#x000a0;handling and disposal of radiopharmaceuticals are crucial to ensure safety and prevent environmental contamination, including wearing appropriate protective clothing, using shielding devices during preparation and administration, and following strict protocols for waste disposal. Compounds used for diagnostic interventions typically emit positrons (beta-plus particles) for PET imaging or gamma rays for single-photon emission computed tomography. In contrast,&#x000a0;agents&#x000a0;that emit electrons (beta-minus particles), Auger electrons, or alpha particles (helium nuclei) are generally used for therapeutic interventions.</p>
        <p>Imaging humans after administering a radiopharmaceutical is known as nuclear medicine, molecular imaging, or biomedical imaging. This approach uses extremely&#x000a0;low concentrations of radiotracers, often in submicron quantities, and is currently used to analyze tissue physiology, detect disease, and monitor treatments. However, with the advent of personalized medicine, new applications continue to emerge.</p>
        <p>Radiotherapeutic agents use the radiation emitted by the nuclide to kill target cells or to provide palliative care. Radiation is toxic to tissues in the body: the brain, spinal cord, kidneys, and bone marrow are especially susceptible. Many radiopharmaceuticals are delivered systemically, meaning that, ideally, they should preferentially target tumor tissue&#x000a0;over normal healthy tissue. Various specific radionuclides are commonly used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28218.s2" sec-type="Issues of Concern">
        <title>Issues of Concern</title>
        <p>Technetium-99m is one of the most commonly used radionuclides. This radionuclide is a gamma emitter primarily used in diagnostic imaging and has limited therapeutic utility. Applications include imaging of the thyroid, lacrimal glands, vascular structures, lungs, bones, and myocardium. Technetium-99m is the most commonly used radionuclide in diagnostic investigations. Many other radionuclides can be used, including but not limited to iodine-123, iodine-131, gallium-67, gallium-68, selenium-75, fluorine-18, strontium-89, thallium-201, and xenon-133.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Iodine-131 is a radioisotope with a broad range of applications.&#x000a0;This&#x000a0;radioisotope&#x000a0;undergoes beta decay, resulting in mutations in the cells it penetrates. This characteristic makes it useful for thyroid ablation, as the thyroid is the only organ in the body that&#x000a0;uses iodine.&#x000a0;Iodine-131&#x000a0;is useful in treating various thyroid malignancies and refractory Graves' disease. Iodine-131 causes mutations in cells that take it up; it is often used at high doses, as low doses can increase the risk of subsequent malignancies (incomplete tissue ablation). Iodine-131 is also a low-dose gamma emitter; however, due to malignancy risk, iodine-123, a relatively pure gamma emitter, is used in nuclear medicine thyroid scans. This preference is due to its shorter half-life (approximately 8 hours) and reduced radiation exposure compared to iodine-131.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Cesium-137 is a radioactive isotope of cesium that undergoes beta decay and gamma emission. The isotope is chemically unstable and reactive; thus, it is not routinely used in diagnostic modalities. Cesium-137 is primarily used medically for low-dose temporary intracavity brachytherapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>Fluorine-18 is a radioisotope of fluorine that primarily undergoes positron emission but also undergoes electron capture. Fluorine-18's primary use is as a radiotracer&#x000a0;in PET scans. Thallium has many isotopes; however, thallium-201 is the most widely used in clinical practice.&#x000a0;Thallium-201 undergoes electron capture, emitting x-rays and photons. This feature makes it useful in imaging studies and is often used in cardiac stress tests to assess myocardial perfusion. Xenon-133 is a radioisotope of xenon that undergoes beta decay. This radioisotope is an inhaled radionuclide used to assess pulmonary function and cerebral blood flow. Rubidium-82 is a radioisotope of rubidium that undergoes both positron and gamma emissions. This radioisotope is primarily administered intravenously&#x000a0;to assess myocardial imaging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Gallium-68 and gallium-67 are isotopes of gallium, with the key difference that gallium-68 is a positron-emitting isotope, typically used in PET scans due to its short half-life, whereas gallium-67 is a gamma-emitting isotope used in standard nuclear medicine scans with a longer half-life.&#x000a0;Although gallium-67 was once widely used for detecting infections and certain cancers, such as lymphoma, its use has largely been phased out. Gallium-68, often combined with a molecule such as DOTATATE, is now frequently used to target specific receptors on neuroendocrine tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r11">[11]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28218.s3" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Radiopharmaceuticals are crucial in healthcare, particularly in imaging, with millions of nuclear medicine procedures conducted annually in the United States. These agents are also vital for treating various malignancies and many benign tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Designing radiopharmaceuticals is a rigorous and complex process that relies on multiple factors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Selecting the appropriate nuclide is critical, as&#x000a0;each nuclide has a specific half-life and decay mode, both of which&#x000a0;impact the localization and utility of the radiopharmaceutical. Other factors, such as molecular stability, ease of use, and production cost, are also important.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Specific radiopharmaceuticals, such as radium-223, help treat painful bone metastases in prostate cancer patients with castration-resistant bone metastases and have been approved by the Food and Drug Administration for this use.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Lutetium-177-PSMA and actinium-225-PSMA are the newer radionuclides used for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;and lutetium-177-dotatate is used to treat gastroenteropancreatic neuroendocrine tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28218.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28218.s4" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>The use of radionuclides involves an interprofessional approach, and many disciplines must work in tandem to safely deliver these agents. Each healthcare team member must understand the critical nature of their role, what it entails, and provide input to ensure accurate diagnostic results while minimizing unnecessary risks to the patient. This collaborative approach ensures that radiopharmaceuticals are used safely and effectively, thereby minimizing risks to both patients and healthcare providers.</p>
        <p>Potential risks include extravasation, in which radiopharmaceuticals are accidentally injected into the soft tissue surrounding the vein, which can harm the patient and result in inaccurate diagnostic or therapeutic outcomes. Such events should be promptly documented, reported, and communicated to the patient.</p>
      </sec>
